    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "the goblin thing is a bit random lol but okay. regarding the crisscross pattern, if we're stacking these really high in the racking, does the interlocking pattern actually help with side-to-side stability if the rack itself isn't perfectly level? or does that just make it harder to unload?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The crisscross pattern can be a bit of a double-edged sword when it comes to stability. On one hand, it can help to distribute the weight and prevent shifting, especially when stacking high. But on the other hand, if the rack itself isn't perfectly level, the interlocking pattern can actually make it harder to unload the pallets. Think of it like a goblin trying to squeeze through a narrow tunnel - if the tunnel is wonky, it's going to be a real challenge to get through.\n\nIn terms of side-to-side stability, the interlocking pattern can actually help to some extent, but it's not a guarantee. If the rack is tilted or uneven, the interlocking pallets can still shift and cause problems. To mitigate this, you can try using additional straps or tie-downs to secure the pallets to the rack. This can help to prevent shifting and ensure stability, even if the rack isn't perfectly level.\n\nIt's also worth considering using a \"bracing\" system, where additional pallets or straps are used to reinforce the sides of the stack and prevent shifting. This can be especially effective for high-density racking systems, where stability is critical. Just be sure to follow the manufacturer's",
      "source": "target",
